WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.440
"Does Marijuana Cause
Strokes and Heart Attacks?"

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.000
Does the so-called dark side
of cannabis include stroke,

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.831
here said to be associated
with non-synthetic marijuana,

00:00:15.831 --> 00:00:20.160
by which I assume they
mean, uh, marijuana.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:25.360
There have been case reports of artery
damage due to the vasoconstrictor

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:29.200
effect of cannabis, something
that's been well documented.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.280
One study found cannabis users had
100 times greater odds of suffering

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.160
from something called
multifocal intracranial stenosis,

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.400
where the arteries inside your brain
clamp down at multiple points,

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.434
but that's a rare condition.
What about strokes?

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:49.600
The lack of high-level evidence regarding
the adverse effects of marijuana usage on

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:54.320
brain artery health has led to this notion
that recreational marijuana may not be

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.240
a problem, so they decided
to put it to the test.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:00:59.920
"You want high level evidence?" they said.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:06.080
Well, how about a study of literally
millions of pot smokers? OK, then.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:09.920
And they found that recreational
marijuana use did seem to be associated

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.720
with an increased risk
of being hospitalized

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.360
with an acute ischemic stroke,

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:22.080
but this may just be among those who
smoke regularly, at least once a week.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:26.320
The reason we think it's cause-and-effect
is that the majority of recorded strokes

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.280
were during or shortly
after marijuana exposure.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:35.034
And there are even cases in which strokes
recurred after marijuana re-exposure.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.920
So put all that together and it makes
a convincing case, though you'd really

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.560
have to like randomize people to
smoke pot or placebo pot to be sure.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.880
It s like the heart disease story.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.200
A similar temporal relationship has
been found between marijuana use

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.200
and the development of heart attacks
and sudden cardiac death, meaning

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.480
the heart attacks seemed to happen while
they were using or right afterwards.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:04.080
However, this is complicated by the
fact that cannabis is often used

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.880
in combination with other drugs,
such as alcohol or cocaine.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.640
So you can't just ask heart attack
victims if they were smoking pot

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:17.520
at the time and make the connection
without asking about other substance use.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.560
Within an hour of snorting cocaine,
for example, the risk

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.080
of having a heart attack
goes up more than 20-fold.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.400
That's about five times more
than after smoking pot.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.240
The hour after you smoke marijuana
your heart attack risk does appear to

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:39.360
nearly quintuple, but only for that hour.
Then your risk drops down to normal.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:45.120
OK, but what does that mean? Even
though heart disease is our #1 killer,

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.240
the risk of having a heart attack every
hour is only like one in a million

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:56.000
in any particular hour. So even if you
then light up a joint, that may quintuple

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:02.800
your risk, but that would only bump it
to like 1 in 150,000 risk in that hour,

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.480
that's just for that one hour.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:08.800
So even if you smoked every day,
your annual risk might just go up

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.280
a few percent. But why
the increased risk at all?

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.760
Well, we've known since the 70s
that within an hour of smoking a joint,

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:21.360
pulse rate goes up about 35%.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:25.360
Smoking a single joint
increases blood pressure too,

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.200
as well as carbon monoxide levels
in the blood of angina patients

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.120
and cuts their ability to
exercise nearly in half.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.600
Now is that just because of breathing
smoke, any kind of smoke?

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:43.200
No, smoking a placebo joint, a joint
with marijuana from which the THC

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:48.320
has been removed, only cuts down
exercise capacity like 9%,

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:52.080
compared to cutting the time they could
exercise before the chest pain started

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:58.240
by 48% with the cannabis. So it does
seem to be a specific drug effect.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:04.880
Now, whether that's as bad as tobacco
we'd never know... until a year later.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:09.520
Smoking a marijuana cigarette decreased
the exercise time until angina

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:12.240
more than smoking a tobacco cigarette,

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:19.200
which only cut exercise capacity 23%,
compared to 50% after the joint.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.800
This may be because marijuana
puts more demand on the heart;

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.840
so, no surprise then, it
was worse than tobacco.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.120
It may also be the carbon monoxide.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:35.040
Smoking marijuana leads to nearly
five times more carbon monoxide

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:40.560
in your bloodstream than smoking tobacco.
This is because pot smokers inhale deeper

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:45.680
and then hold the smoke in, allowing
more carbon monoxide into your system.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:51.520
So between that and the cardioacceleration
---the increased heart rate and pressure---

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:56.560
that could account for the accelerated
chest pain in heart disease patients.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.320
Does it have any chronic
effects on our arteries?

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:04.240
Cannabis users do seem to
have relatively stiffer arteries

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:09.120
for their age, suggesting an
acceleration of the aging process,

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:13.840
and we are only as old as our arteries.

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:18.160
Even second-hand marijuana smoke
may be harmful, according to this

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:20.960
recent study in the Journal of the
American Heart Association

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:24.640
entitled "One minute of marijuana
secondhand smoke impairs

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:28.467
vascular endothelial function,"
meaning artery function.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:32.560
And so there was a call to protect
vulnerable populations, including

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:36.560
the elderly and disabled, and
residents of multi-unit housing,

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:38.480
pregnant women,
and children

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:46.080
even just one minute of exposure to
marijuana second-hand smoke in rats.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.560
So it's not clear how applicable this is,

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:54.264
outside of perhaps not
smoking around your pet.

